{
  "scenario": "Operation Quiet Invoice, the STIX bundle's fictional phishing campaign against a UK building society, read through ATT&CK and D3FEND. The bundle's four attack patterns and the Rclone tool are ATT&CK's own objects, the same by STIX id; the techniques it lacks in the ATT&CK sample, Phishing, Spearphishing Attachment, User Execution, Malicious File, Exfiltration Over Web Service and Exfiltration to Cloud Storage, are added from ATT&CK Enterprise 20.1 with their tactics, Rclone, and the mitigations Restrict Web-Based Content and Execution Prevention. Those techniques, tactics and mitigations are the same as D3FEND's, by ATT&CK id and name, which leads to the defensive techniques D3FEND relates to them; and the email, attachment, DLL, URL, processes and traffic the bundle observed are instances of the D3FEND digital artifacts that the defensive techniques analyse, filter or isolate.",
